A||||16||FLASHY||Taillight cleaning...What a difference!!  (pics)||||14||
Z||000000||FLASHY||09-21-2001||08:04 PM||||Whenever I'd wash my car it bugged me how the taillights would still look dirty. Then I was told dust builds up on the inside and needs to be cleaned. Well I finally did that and couldn't believe the improvement. Now all I need to do is polish the outside and they'll look brand new. Take a look at the difference.<P><BR>Here's one of them taken apart and ready for a bath.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tailclean1_1.jpg"> <P>Here's the before pic.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tailclean2_1.jpg"> <BR>And now the after pic.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tailclean3_1.jpg"> <P><BR>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/flash_sig.gif">  <P>"I know you believe you understand what you think I wrote, but I'm not sure you realize that what you read is not what I really meant"<p>[This message has been edited by FLASHY (edited 09-21-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000001||banditbalz||09-21-2001||08:23 PM||||Looks great... I did mine about 7 years ago and forgot what a difference it made. What's going on with your tailpipes? One outlet looks longer than the other!<BR>_<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/blackwhite2_1.jpg"> <P><P>------------------<BR>Silver 86 GT<BR>completely stock,<BR>completely fast!||||reg||1
Z||000002||FLASHY||09-21-2001||08:28 PM||||Actually both outer pipes don't have chrome extention pipes on them because they were aiming a bit downward and WAY outward. It looked even sillier than it does now. Once I get new exhaust that'll all be fixed.||||reg||1
Z||000003||FLASHY||09-21-2001||08:31 PM||||Oh about the one outlet looking longer than the other...it's just the perspective. They're the same length.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cheever3000||09-21-2001||09:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I was told dust builds up on the inside and needs to be cleaned. Well I finally did that and couldn't believe the improvement.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE>So, how exactly did you do it?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||FLASHY||09-21-2001||09:43 PM||||Well you open the trunk, pop off those six plastic caps above the taillights. Take out the six screws and gently pull away the lenses to see in behind. Unhook all the light bulbs. Now you have the tailllights detached from the car. Next, you'll notice the tabs that are holding everything in place. Pull the tabs away and slowly peel the backing apart from the face of the taillight. I say "peel" because they are glued together with some rubberized caulking. It peels apart quite easily. Then remove all the internal parts and wash them. I glued them back shut by removing the excess glue from before and added new black silicone. Then press them back together until the tabs snap together. Then hook up the light bulbs and screw them back into place. Simple.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cheever3000||09-21-2001||09:46 PM||||I meant the washing part.  You just used like Dawn or something?  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||FLASHY||09-21-2001||09:50 PM||||Sorry Cheever, I used liquid dish soap. And make sure you use very soft cloths because the lenses scratch easily.||||reg||11
Z||000008||FLASHY||09-21-2001||09:59 PM||||I'd better correct myself. The two inner tail pipes are the same length and the two outer are the same length but shorter than the inner ones.....uh...ya.||||reg||1
Z||000009||formula2||09-21-2001||11:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I<BR>                    glued them back shut by removing the excess glue from before and added new black<BR>                    silicone.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If you have used silicone,  it is now sealed for ever....||||reg||2
Z||000010||Fformula88||09-21-2001||11:24 PM||||I am glad it worked for you and that it made a difference!  I have never touched my Formula's tail lights since every set I have tried to take apart have ended up cracking on me!  Those things sure get brittle with age!||||reg||1
Z||000011||Spektrum-87GT||09-22-2001||12:12 AM||||my tailights are in desperate need of this, anyone have a good glue to use? i dont want to seal it forever... so if i need to do it again i'm not going to have to break out the saw and hack it to death||||reg||1
Z||000012||MinnGreenGT||09-22-2001||12:22 AM||||I was told by my brother that DAP makes a new caulk/sealer that creates a temporary seal (much like the existing one).  when you first seal it, it holds well- if you split the seam... it'll peel right off.  Check around for it at the local hardware store (I'm thinking Home Depot will have something along those lines).  Sorry I don't have a cost or part number or anything- just word of mouth.  BTW: I REALLY need to clean mine!  They've never been cleaned- ever- and I lived on a gravel road with the car for 5 years.  Yuck.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/halfpole.gif">  <BR>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett||||reg||1
Z||000013||qwikgta||09-22-2001||12:39 AM||||I siliconed my first pair together, when I went to make a new mod, i could not get them apart, DON'T use it unless you never want to get back in.  I ended up braking them and they were my good pair.  Now I have to use my backup.<P>Rob||||reg||1
Z||000014||FLASHY||09-22-2001||04:54 AM||||I guess I'll find out the hard way if it can come apart again. I didn't think it would be that tough. Dang!||||reg||1
Z||000015||rogergarrison||09-22-2001||09:57 AM||||hold them together and add a bead of silicon around the edge. It will still hold fine and if you ever need back inside, just cut the bead with a razorblade.||||reg||1
Z||000016||deceler8||09-22-2001||11:09 AM||||last time I took a set apart, the stuff holding them together looked like rope caulk, which you can get at about any home improvement or hardware store. <P>Rope caulk is designed for temporary use and it stays soft but seals well.||||reg||1
